---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import FaviconGenerator from '@/components/tools/generators/FaviconGenerator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="favicon-generator">
  <FaviconGenerator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to generate a favicon pack</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop in a square image. An SVG or a PNG of 512 px or more gives the best results.</li>
      <li>Add a background colour if your logo is transparent. iOS paints transparency black on the home screen, so a solid background is usually better.</li>
      <li>Add a little padding if the mark is dense; about 10% keeps a wordmark from touching the edges.</li>
      <li>Check the real-size previews. If the 16 px version is a smudge, the mark has too much detail for a browser tab.</li>
      <li>Download the ZIP, unpack it into your site root, and paste the HTML snippet into your <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The favicon generator renders each image from a 1024 px master by repeated halving, not one large
      downscale, which keeps the 16 and 32 px versions readable. All work happens in this tab, and your logo
      is not uploaded.
    </p>

    <h2>What each file is for</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>File</th>
            <th>Size</th>
            <th>Consumed by</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>favicon.ico</code></td><td>16, 32, 48</td><td>Legacy browsers, Windows shortcuts, and the default request to <code>/favicon.ico</code> when nothing else matches</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>favicon-16x16.png</code></td><td>16</td><td>Browser tab and bookmarks bar at 1× density</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>favicon-32x32.png</code></td><td>32</td><td>Retina tab, taskbar shortcut, most browser UI</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>favicon-48x48.png</code></td><td>48</td><td>Windows site shortcuts</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>favicon-64x64.png</code></td><td>64</td><td>High-DPI tabs and some extension surfaces</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>favicon-128x128.png</code></td><td>128</td><td>Chrome Web Store, packaged apps</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>apple-touch-icon.png</code></td><td>180</td><td>iOS and iPadOS “Add to Home Screen”</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>android-chrome-192x192.png</code></td><td>192</td><td>Android home screen</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>android-chrome-512x512.png</code></td><td>512</td><td>Android splash screen and PWA install dialog</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>site.webmanifest</code></td><td>n/a</td><td>PWA name, icons, theme colour, install behaviour</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>The HTML you need</h2>
    <p>
      Five links and one meta tag cover all current browsers and platforms. The many
      <code>msapplication-*</code> tags and Apple size variants that older generators write have not been
      necessary for years.
    </p>
    <pre><code>&lt;link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="any"&gt;
&lt;link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="16x16" href="/favicon-16x16.png"&gt;
&lt;link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="32x32" href="/favicon-32x32.png"&gt;
&lt;link rel="apple-touch-icon" sizes="180x180" href="/apple-touch-icon.png"&gt;
&lt;link rel="manifest" href="/site.webmanifest"&gt;
&lt;meta name="theme-color" content="#0f172a"&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      If you also have an SVG favicon, add
      <code>&lt;link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg"&gt;</code> before the PNG lines.
      Browsers that support SVG use the vector; the others use the PNGs.
    </p>

    <h2>Inside an .ico file</h2>
    <p>
      ICO is a container, not an image format. It starts with a six-byte ICONDIR header (two reserved bytes,
      a type field of 1 for icons, and a count), followed by one sixteen-byte directory entry per image. Each
      entry records the width and height as single bytes (0 means 256), the colour depth, the payload length
      and the byte offset where the payload starts. The payloads follow, one after another.
    </p>
    <p>
      Originally each payload was a headerless BMP with an awkward AND mask for transparency. Since Windows
      Vista, the payload can be a complete PNG file, which is smaller and has real alpha. This tool writes PNG
      payloads at 16, 32 and 48 pixels, and all browsers and supported Windows versions read them. The tool
      builds the whole container in JavaScript in this tab, with no server-side image processing.
    </p>

    <h2>Designing a mark that survives 16 pixels</h2>
    <p>
      A favicon is 16 device pixels on a standard display. That is enough for one bold shape or one letter,
      and nothing else. Some rules:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Drop the wordmark. Use the symbol, or one initial. Text of more than about four characters is illegible at 16 px.</li>
      <li>Make the strokes thicker. A hairline that looks elegant at 512 px disappears at 16.</li>
      <li>Increase the contrast. Subtle tonal differences merge into one grey when the image is a few pixels.</li>
      <li>Test on both themes. A dark navy mark disappears against dark browser chrome. If yours does, add a second SVG favicon with a <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> media query in it.</li>
      <li>Keep the silhouette distinctive. At tab size, people recognise shape and colour, never detail.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why your new favicon is not showing</h2>
    <p>
      Browsers cache favicons more aggressively than almost any other file, often in a cache separate from the
      normal page cache. The causes, most likely first: your browser keeps the old one (open the icon URL
      directly and hard-reload it), a CDN keeps it (purge the path), or a service worker serves an old copy. A
      query string such as <code>/favicon.ico?v=2</code> in the link tags fixes all layers at once.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
